Thanks everyone for your input. If you look at the source code for the
wrapper function used the Stage2  in metaSEM package by running
`metaSEM::tssem2`, you'll see that it uses "out <- wls(..., n =
sum(tssem1.obj$n))" where `n` in `tssem1.obj$n` comes from Stage1
(i.e., pooled correlations) using `metaSEM::tssem1`. In metaSEM
package (like lavaan), the assumption is that data is in wide-format
and each study has one, unique "n".

So, as you can see above, in wls() "n"s from each study are summed,
assuming a wide-format dataset.

However, my question is given that my data is in long-format, because
I pooled my data in Stage 1 using a multilevel model, shall I use one
"n" from each study and then sum them to mimic the "wls(..., n =
sum(tssem1.obj$n))"?

I would think the answer is a yes.

Simon

   study var1 var2     yi  ni
       1 acog perf -0.55 142
       1 asom perf -0.48 142
.
.
.
      38 acog conf -0.68  30
      38 asom conf -0.71  30

