Dear Simon,

One way is to conduct a sensitivity analysis by using different sample sizes. If the results are similar, the choice has limited impact on the conclusions.

Best,
Mike

On Sat, Aug 5, 2023 at 1:12 PM Simon Harmel <sim.h...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Dear Mike,
>
> Thank you very much for your response. If I want to follow the same
> multiple-group SEM tradition, then I should get one "n" from each
> study (and perhaps if a study has more than one unique "n" in it, I
> will use harmonic mean, or min "n" in the study etc.), and then sum
> them in wls(), is that correct?
>
> Also, given that the sum of the "n" from each study, "may not
> necessarily be the best choice" and may impact some of the fit
> indices, I wonder if there is any other fit evaluation index you might
> recommend in this situation?
>
> Thanks,
> Simon
>
> On Fri, Aug 4, 2023 at 11:01 PM Mike Cheung <mikewl...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Hi Simon,
> >
> > When using metaSEM::wls(), the weight in the analysis is determined by the inverse of the sampling covariance matrix of the pooled correlation matrix. It's important to note that the chi-square statistic, its p-value, and the standard errors of the parameter estimates are not affected by the choice of sample size. However, the sample size affects fit indices that involve sample size in their calculations, such as RMSEA and CFI.
> >
> > The metaSEM::wls() uses the total sample size as the sample size, which follows the multiple-group SEM tradition. However, it may not necessarily be the best choice.
> >
> > Best,
> > Mike
> >
> > On Sat, Aug 5, 2023 at 6:59 AM Simon Harmel <sim.h...@gmail.com> wrote:
